I've talked to ChatGPT about physics, a topic I don't know much about, except that a few things like air lift, kinetic vs static friction, osmosis, and the stuff listed in <https://xkcd.com/2682/> (how paracetamol/tylenol works? why baloons electrify hair?) are somewhat "controversial" in that Wikipedia, Quora and stackexchange and other places provide various conflicting answers.

I've also talked to it about the topic I a know a bit better: C++ std::atomic's semantics.

In both conversations ChatGPT was acting very self-confident. I must say I felt quite persuaded by it when talking about physics. I could tell the information it gave me about C++ was inaccurate, though. Now, how should I combine these two observations?

I think an AI which gives confident wrong answers to people who ask it about topics they themselves are not experts about is dangerous.

Physics
=======

**How does a plane generate lift?**

A plane generates lift by using its wings to manipulate the air around it. As the plane moves forward, air flows over and under the wings. The shape of the wings is designed to cause the air moving over the top of the wing to move faster than the air moving under the wing. This difference in speed creates a difference in pressure, with the higher pressure air under the wing pushing the plane upward. This upward force is known as lift and it is what allows a plane to fly.

**Are you sure of this explanation?**

Yes, I am confident in the explanation I provided. The basic principles of flight, including lift, have been well understood for many years, and are studied in great detail in the fields of aerodynamics and aviation. I am a large language model trained by OpenAI, and my purpose is to provide accurate and helpful information on a wide range of topics. If you have any further questions or concerns, I will do my best to address them.

**So is it the lift caused by Bernoulli's effect?**

Yes, the lift generated by an airplane wing is typically caused by the Bernoulli effect. This principle states that as the speed of a fluid (such as air) increases, the pressure within the fluid decreases. The wing of an airplane is designed to cause the air moving over the top of the wing to move faster than the air moving under the wing, which creates a difference in pressure and generates lift. This is just one of several factors that contribute to the generation of lift, and other effects such as the flow of air around the wing also play a role.

**What about Conda's effect?**

I'm sorry, but I'm not familiar with a "Conda" effect in relation to the generation of lift in airplanes. As I mentioned earlier, the lift generated by an airplane wing is typically caused by the Bernoulli effect, which describes the relationship between the speed of a fluid and its pressure. There are many other factors that also contribute to the generation of lift, and the study of these factors is a complex and ongoing area of research in the fields of aerodynamics and aviation. If you have any further questions, I will do my best to answer them.

**I'm sorry, this was a typo. I meant Coanda Effect.**

Ah, I see. The Coanda effect, also known as the Coanda phenomenon, is a common phenomenon in fluid dynamics that describes the tendency of a fluid jet to follow the contours of a nearby surface. This effect can be observed in many everyday situations, such as the way that water flows out of a faucet or the way that smoke flows from a chimney. In the case of an airplane wing, the Coanda effect can play a role in the generation of lift by helping to control the flow of air around the wing and improving its overall aerodynamic performance. However, the Coanda effect is just one of many factors that contribute to the generation of lift in airplanes, and the lift generated by an airplane wing is primarily due to the Bernoulli effect, as I explained earlier.

**The reason I ask, is because I've seen people in the internet claiming that there are widespread misconceptions in how Bernoulli effect applies to air lift...**

Yes, there are many misconceptions about the role of the Bernoulli effect in the generation of lift in airplanes. This is a complex topic, and it is important to understand that the Bernoulli effect is just one of many factors that contribute to the generation of lift. The lift generated by an airplane wing is the result of a combination of factors, including the shape of the wing, the speed at which the plane is moving, and the flow of air around the wing. The Bernoulli effect plays a role in the generation of lift by creating a difference in pressure between the top and bottom of the wing, but other factors are also involved. It is important to understand the full range of factors that contribute to lift in order to have a complete and accurate understanding of how airplanes fly.

**Does it take equal time for the air to go above and below the wing?**

The time it takes for air to pass over and under an airplane wing is not the same. The air moving over the top of the wing typically moves faster than the air moving under the wing, due to the shape of the wing and the way it is positioned in relation to the direction of flight. This difference in speed is one of the factors that contributes to the generation of lift, as the faster-moving air above the wing creates a lower pressure area that helps to lift the plane upward. However, the time it takes for air to pass over and under the wing is not the primary factor that determines the amount of lift generated. Instead, the lift generated by an airplane wing is largely determined by the shape of the wing, the speed of the plane, and the flow of air around the wing.

