**I ask because authors of this paper claim that water can sometimes flow towards a higher concentration of water. What could be an explanation of this phenomenon?**

It is true that in some cases, water can flow through a semipermeable membrane towards a higher concentration of water. This phenomenon, known as reverse osmosis, is possible when a pressure is applied to the side of the membrane with the higher concentration of water. This pressure creates a concentration gradient that is stronger than the concentration gradient of the solute, and it overcomes the natural tendency of the water molecules to move from the region of low solute concentration to the region of high solute concentration. As a result, the water molecules are forced to move through the membrane towards the side with the higher concentration of water. Reverse osmosis is a complex process that requires specialized equipment and conditions, and it is not the same as the more common phenomenon of osmosis. However, it is an important process that has many practical applications, including the desalination of seawater and the purification of drinking water.

