C++ atomics:
============

**do you understand C++ memory ordering rules?**

Yes, I am familiar with C++ memory ordering rules. In C++, memory ordering specifies the order in which memory accesses are executed by a processor or a compiler, and it can affect the behavior of a program. Memory ordering rules are used to ensure that memory accesses are executed in a predictable and consistent manner, which is important for ensuring the correctness of concurrent programs. There are several different memory ordering rules in C++, including sequential consistency, relaxed memory ordering, and release consistency, which each have their own specific rules for how memory accesses are ordered.

**Great! So, suppose I have two atomic integers:**  
`**std::atomic<int> x,y;**`  
**and I'd like to store 7 to x, and 13 to y, but I want to do so in such a way, that if another thread loads 7 from x then it is guaranteed to also see the 13 I've stored to y. How do I achieve this in C++?**

To achieve this in C++, you can use the memory\_order\_seq\_cst memory ordering rule, which ensures that memory accesses are executed in a sequentially consistent manner. This means that if one thread stores a value to an atomic variable using memory\_order\_seq\_cst, and another thread subsequently loads that same value from the atomic variable using memory\_order\_seq\_cst, the second thread is guaranteed to see the value that was stored by the first thread.

Here is an example of how you could store 7 to x and 13 to y in a way that ensures that another thread will see the 13 when it loads 7 from x:


```
std::atomic<int> x,y;

// Store 7 to x and 13 to y in a sequentially consistent manner
x.store(7, std::memory_order_seq_cst);
y.store(13, std::memory_order_seq_cst);
```
Note that using memory\_order\_seq\_cst can be somewhat expensive, as it requires the use of a global memory fence. If performance is a concern, you may want to use a weaker memory ordering rule, such as memory\_order\_release and memory\_order\_acquire, which can provide similar guarantees but with less overhead.

**are you sure this would work?**

Yes, I am confident that using the memory\_order\_seq\_cst memory ordering rule would work as described. memory\_order\_seq\_cst ensures that memory accesses are executed in a sequentially consistent manner, which means that if one thread stores a value to an atomic variable using memory\_order\_seq\_cst, and another thread subsequently loads that same value from the atomic variable using memory\_order\_seq\_cst, the second thread is guaranteed to see the value that was stored by the first thread.

However, it is important to note that using memory\_order\_seq\_cst can be somewhat expensive, as it requires the use of a global memory fence. If performance is a concern, you may want to consider using a weaker memory ordering rule, such as memory\_order\_release and memory\_order\_acquire, which can provide similar guarantees but with less overhead.

**I think your code has a bug. What if another thread reads x right after**  
`**x.store(7, std::memory\_order\_seq\_cst);**`  
**but before**  
`**y.store(13, std::memory\_order\_seq\_cst);**`  
**?**

You are correct, there is a potential race condition in the code that I provided. If another thread reads the value of x after the first thread has stored 7 to x, but before the first thread has stored 13 to y, then the second thread will not see the value of 13 that was stored to y.

To avoid this race condition, you can use a C++ std::atomic\_flag to provide mutual exclusion between the two threads. This will ensure that the second thread cannot read the value of x until after the first thread has stored 13 to y. Here is an example of how you could do this:


```
std::atomic<int> x,y;
std::atomic_flag lock = ATOMIC_FLAG_INIT;

// Acquire the lock
while (lock.test_and_set(std::memory_order_acquire))
   ; // Spin

// Store 7 to x and 13 to y in a sequentially consistent manner
x.store(7, std::memory_order_seq_cst);
y.store(13, std::memory_order_seq_cst);

// Release the lock
lock.clear(std::memory_order_release);
```
In this example, the first thread acquires the lock using test\_and\_set, which sets the flag and returns its previous value. The while loop will spin until the lock is available, at which point the first thread will enter the critical section and store 7 to x and 13 to y using the memory\_order\_seq\_cst memory ordering rule. After the values have been stored, the first thread will release the lock using clear, which allows another thread to enter the critical section and read the values of x and y. Because the memory\_order\_seq\_cst memory ordering rule is used, the second thread is guaranteed to see the values of 7 and 13 that were stored by the first thread.

Note that using an std::atomic\_flag for mutual exclusion is just one way to avoid the race condition in the original code. There are other ways to achieve the same result, such as using a std::mutex or a std::atomic<bool> for the lock. The specific method that you choose will depend on your specific requirements and the tradeoffs that you are willing to make in terms of performance and complexity.

**Could we eliminate the race condition by simply storing 13 to y before we attempt to store 7 to x?**

Yes, storing the value of 13 to y before storing the value of 7 to x would eliminate the race condition in the original code. This is because the second thread would not be able to read the value of x until after the value of 13 has been stored to y, which means that the second thread would always see the value of 13 when it reads the value of x.

Here is an example of how you could modify the original code to eliminate the race condition:


```
std::atomic<int> x,y;

// Store 13 to y in a sequentially consistent manner
y.store(13, std::memory_order_seq_cst);

// Store 7 to x in a sequentially consistent manner
x.store(7, std::memory_order_seq_cst);
```
In this example, the first thread first stores the value of 13 to y using the memory\_order\_seq\_cst memory ordering rule. This ensures that the second thread will see the value of 13 when it reads the value of y. The first thread then stores the value of 7 to x using the memory\_order\_seq\_cst memory ordering rule. Because the second thread cannot read the value of x until after the value of 13 has been stored to y, the second thread is guaranteed to see the values of 7 and 13 when it reads the values of x and y.

Note that using this approach has the disadvantage of potentially reducing performance, as the first thread must store the value of 13 to y before it can store the value of 7 to x. This means that the first thread must wait for the value of 13 to be stored to y before it can continue, which can introduce a delay. In contrast, using an std::atomic\_flag for mutual exclusion allows the first thread to store the values of 7 and 13 to x and y concurrently, which can potentially improve performance. The specific approach that you choose will depend on your specific requirements and the tradeoffs that you are willing to make in terms of performance and complexity.

