No, using the memory\_order\_relaxed memory ordering rule would not work as intended in this case. The memory\_order\_relaxed memory ordering rule provides the weakest guarantees of all the memory ordering rules in C++. It allows memory accesses to be executed in any order, and does not provide any guarantees about the visibility of memory accesses to other threads.

In the case where y.store(13, std::memory\_order\_relaxed) is used, there is no guarantee that the second thread will see the value of 13 when it reads the value of x. This is because the memory\_order\_relaxed memory ordering rule allows the compiler and processor to reorder memory accesses, and does not provide any guarantees about the visibility of memory accesses to other threads.

In order to ensure that the second thread sees the value of 13 when it reads the value of x, you must use a stronger memory ordering rule, such as memory\_order\_seq\_cst or memory\_order\_release and memory\_order\_acquire. These memory ordering rules provide stronger guarantees about the order in which memory accesses are executed and the visibility of memory accesses to other threads, which ensures that the second thread will see the value of 13 when it reads the value of x.
